\title{Local Propagation and Weighted Harmonic Uniqueness\\in Claw-Free Cubic Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 16, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Zero forcing is a monotone coloring process on a graph: a colored vertex with exactly one uncolored neighbor colors that neighbor. We develop the local and algebraic mechanisms that make this process useful in claw-free cubic graphs. Every force increases the colored cardinality by one; reachability is monotone and antisymmetric; and triangle and diamond units admit explicit propagation certificates. Given arbitrary nonzero directed edge weights over any field, a weighted harmonic function vanishing on a zero forcing set must vanish identically. The proof identifies the common mechanism behind combinatorial forcing and linear uniqueness: at a forcing vertex all terms but one in the neighbor equation vanish. We also prove that a dominating set in maximum degree three has size at least one quarter of the graph order, that every finite cubic graph has even order, and that every partition into triangle and diamond units contains an even number of triangle units. Algorithms and examples illustrate the results and isolate a foundation for sharper bounds under additional global hypotheses.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph. Begin with a set $S\subseteq V$ of colored vertices. A colored vertex $u$ may color an uncolored neighbor $w$ when $w$ is the only uncolored neighbor of $u$. A set from which repeated legal moves color every vertex is a \emph{zero forcing set}; its minimum cardinality is the \emph{zero forcing number} $Z(G)$.

The definition combines local determinism with global optimization. It is especially natural in cubic graphs, where every vertex has degree $3$, and claw-free graphs, which have no induced $K_{1,3}$. In a claw-free cubic graph the three neighbors of a vertex cannot be pairwise nonadjacent. Local clustering is unavoidable, making triangles and diamonds natural transmission units.

There is also a linear interpretation. Put a nonzero directed weight on every edge incidence and impose a homogeneous weighted neighbor-sum equation at every vertex. If a solution vanishes on the colored set and a force occurs, all but one term of the equation at the forcing vertex vanish. The final nonzero coefficient then forces the remaining value to vanish. A forcing chain is therefore both a coloring certificate and a uniqueness certificate for a family of linear systems.

We distinguish carefully between local conclusions and sharp numerical bounds requiring global structure. Cubicity and claw-freeness motivate unit decompositions but do not alone guarantee a Hamiltonian contraction network or a corresponding extremal formula.

\section{Definitions}
\begin{definition}
For $u\in V$, let $N(u)=\{v\in V:u\sim v\}$ and $\deg(u)=|N(u)|$. The graph is \emph{cubic} if $\deg(u)=3$ for all $u$. It is \emph{claw-free} if it has no induced copy of $K_{1,3}$. A \emph{triangle} consists of three pairwise adjacent vertices. A \emph{diamond} is $K_4$ with one edge deleted.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{legal force} from a colored set $S$ is an ordered pair $u\to w$ satisfying $u\in S$, $w\notin S$, $u\sim w$, and
\[
 z\sim u\text{ and }z\notin S\quad\Longrightarrow\quad z=w.
\]
The new state is $S\cup\{w\}$. A \emph{forcing sequence} is a finite chain of legal forces, with a length-zero chain allowed. A set is \emph{zero forcing} if the full vertex set is reachable from it.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A set $D\subseteq V$ is \emph{dominating} if every vertex is in $D$ or adjacent to a vertex in $D$. The least size of such a set is $\gamma(G)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Let $K$ be a field and assign $A_{uv}\in K$ to every ordered adjacent pair $(u,v)$, with $A_{uv}\ne0$. A function $x:V\to K$ is \emph{weighted harmonic} if
\[
 \sum_{v\in N(u)}A_{uv}x(v)=0
\]
for every $u\in V$. The weights need not be symmetric or positive.
\end{definition}

\section{The order structure of forcing}
\begin{theorem}[Strict growth]
If one legal force takes $S$ to $T$, then $|T|=|S|+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By definition $T=S\cup\{w\}$ with $w\notin S$, so exactly one new element is inserted.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sequence length]
A forcing sequence of $m$ moves from $S$ to $T$ satisfies $|T|=|S|+m$. Every successful sequence from $S$ has exactly $|V|-|S|$ moves.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Sum the unit cardinality increase over all moves.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity]
If $T$ is reachable from $S$, then $S\subseteq T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each move inserts a vertex and removes none. Induction on the sequence length proves the inclusion.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Antisymmetry]
If $T$ is reachable from $S$ and $S$ is reachable from $T$, then $S=T$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Monotonicity gives both inclusions.
\end{proof}
Thus every nontrivial transition raises cardinality, and the directed state graph has no nontrivial directed cycle.

\section{Triangle and diamond certificates}
Small motifs become useful only when their boundary conditions make a neighbor unique.

\begin{theorem}[Triangle propagation]
Let $a\in S$ and $b\notin S$. If $a\sim b$ and every uncolored neighbor of $a$ equals $b$, then one legal force reaches $S\cup\{b\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
These assumptions are precisely the color-change rule with forcing vertex $a$ and target $b$.
\end{proof}
In a triangle, the third vertex often supplies an already colored neighbor, while the external neighborhood determines legality. The shape alone is not sufficient.

\begin{theorem}[Diamond propagation]
Suppose $a\in S$, $d\notin S$, and $d$ is the unique uncolored neighbor of $a$. Suppose further that after coloring $d$, a vertex $b$ is uncolored, adjacent to $d$, and is the unique uncolored neighbor of $d$. Then $S\cup\{d,b\}$ is reachable from $S$ in two moves.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
First perform $a\to d$, reaching $S\cup\{d\}$. In that new state the stated uniqueness condition permits $d\to b$.
\end{proof}
This models passage through the spine of a diamond and is compositional across larger unit networks.

\section{Weighted harmonic propagation}
\begin{lemma}[One-step preservation of vanishing]
Let all directed edge weights be nonzero and let $x$ be weighted harmonic. If $x$ vanishes on $S$ and a legal force takes $S$ to $T$, then $x$ vanishes on $T$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write the force as $u\to w$, so $T=S\cup\{w\}$. Harmonicity at $u$ gives
\[
0=\sum_{v\in N(u)}A_{uv}x(v).
\]
Every neighbor $v\ne w$ lies in $S$, because $w$ is the unique uncolored neighbor. All corresponding values vanish, reducing the equation to $A_{uw}x(w)=0$. Since $A_{uw}\ne0$ in a field, $x(w)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing along a forcing sequence]
If $T$ is reachable from $S$ and a weighted harmonic function vanishes on $S$, then it vanishes on $T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on the sequence. The length-zero case is immediate, and the successor case is the preceding lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-forcing weighted harmonic uniqueness]
Let $G$ be any finite simple graph, $K$ any field, and $A$ any nonzero directed edge weighting. If $S$ is zero forcing and $x:V\to K$ is weighted harmonic with $x|_S=0$, then $x=0$ on all of $V$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The full vertex set is reachable from $S$. Apply vanishing along the forcing sequence with $T=V$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Injective restriction]
Let $\mathcal H_A$ be the vector space of weighted harmonic functions. If $S$ is zero forcing, restriction $\mathcal H_A\to K^S$ is injective. Hence
\[
\dim_K\mathcal H_A\le |S|,
\qquad \dim_K\mathcal H_A\le Z(G).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The difference of two harmonic functions agreeing on $S$ is harmonic and vanishes there, hence vanishes globally. The dimension bound follows from injection into $K^S$.
\end{proof}

The theorem uses neither cubicity nor claw-freeness once a forcing chain is supplied. Nonzero weights are essential: a zero coefficient on the forced edge would make the local equation silent about the new value. The field assumption is likewise material; over a ring with zero divisors a nonzero coefficient may annihilate a nonzero value.

\section{Domination in degree three}
\begin{theorem}[Closed-neighborhood bound]
If $\Delta(G)\le3$ and $D$ is dominating, then
\[
 |V|\le4|D|,
\]
so $\gamma(G)\ge\lceil |V|/4\rceil$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each closed neighborhood $N[u]$ with $u\in D$ contains at most four vertices. Since these closed neighborhoods cover $V$,
\[
|V|=\left|\bigcup_{u\in D}N[u]\right|
\le\sum_{u\in D}|N[u]|\le4|D|.
\]
\end{proof}
Domination is static coverage; forcing is directional propagation. The theorem gives a baseline for comparing their local costs on a common triangle--diamond decomposition.

\section{Parity of cubic unit networks}
\begin{theorem}[Cubic parity]
Every finite cubic graph has even order.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The handshaking identity gives
\[
3|V|=\sum_{v\in V}\deg(v)=2|E|.
\]
Thus $3|V|$ is even. Since $3$ is odd, $|V|$ is even.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Triangle-unit parity]
Suppose the vertices of a finite cubic graph are partitioned into $T$ three-vertex units and $D$ four-vertex units. Then $T$ is even.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The partition gives $|V|=3T+4D$. Both $|V|$ and $4D$ are even, so $3T$ is even. Therefore $T$ is even.
\end{proof}
Only unit sizes and cubicity enter this argument. The result provides an immediate consistency test and explains why quantities involving $T/2$ are integral in triangle--diamond decompositions.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Greedy forcing closure}
Store the colored set and, for each colored vertex, its number of uncolored neighbors. Queue every colored vertex whose count is one. Pop a vertex $u$, identify its unique uncolored neighbor $w$, record $u\to w$, color $w$, and update counts adjacent to $w$. Each vertex is colored once and each edge is updated only constantly many times, giving $O(|V|+|E|)$ time and space. The output is either a complete forcing certificate or a stalled closure.

\subsection{Harmonic certificate propagation}
Process a forcing certificate in order. At $u\to w$, verify uniqueness, $A_{uw}\ne0$, and certified zero values on the other neighbors. The equation at $u$ certifies $x(w)=0$. Direct scans cost $O(m\Delta)$ for $m$ moves; maintained counts give linear total complexity.

\subsection{Exact search for small graphs}
Enumerate subsets in increasing size and compute each forcing closure. The first successful cardinality is $Z(G)$. The worst case examines $2^{|V|}$ subsets, with polynomial work per subset. Symmetry reduction and dynamic programming on a contraction graph can improve practice.

\section{Examples}
\begin{example}[Path]
On $P_6$ the singleton $\{0\}$ forces by
\[
0\to1,\quad1\to2,\quad2\to3,\quad3\to4,\quad4\to5.
\]
The five moves equal $6-1$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Triangle and complete graph]
In $K_3$, two vertices force the third, while one cannot force; hence $Z(K_3)=2$. In $K_4$, three vertices force the fourth, while two leave every colored vertex with two uncolored neighbors; hence $Z(K_4)=3$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Weighted path]
Assign arbitrary nonzero directed weights to $P_6$. If a harmonic function vanishes at the endpoint $0$, the displayed forcing sequence propagates vanishing to every vertex. No equality or positivity among weights is needed.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Unit parity]
For $T=4$ and $D=3$, the order is $3\cdot4+4\cdot3=24$, consistent with cubicity. For $T=3$ and $D=3$, the proposed order is $21$, so no cubic graph can have such a partition.
\end{example}

\section{Applications and limitations}
A zero forcing set is a sensor set for the harmonic solution space: measurements there determine the global state. In sparse matrix language, the kernel of every matrix having the prescribed nonzero graph pattern has dimension at most the size of a zero forcing set. In diagnosis or constraint propagation, a force is elimination of the last unknown adjacent to a resolved node.

The local results do not assert a sharp global formula from claw-freeness and cubicity alone. Bounds expressed through counts of triangle and diamond units require a valid decomposition, and Hamiltonian-contraction arguments require the corresponding cyclic global structure. Likewise, the domination estimate is intentionally coarse because closed neighborhoods may overlap.

\section{Future directions}
One direction seeks symmetric real edge weights on diamond-free triangle networks with Hamiltonian cyclic contraction so that harmonic nullity reaches the zero forcing number. The uniqueness theorem supplies one inequality; explicit independent modes are needed for the reverse direction.

A second direction compares domination and forcing by assigning finite boundary states and local costs to triangle and diamond units. A finite-state optimization may reveal sharp asymptotic separation and periodic extremizers.

A third direction studies stability: if deleting at most $r$ unit-vertices leaves a spanning cycle, can each defect be repaired with one additional seed? The local certificates make the proposed cost testable.

Finally, one may seek the converse characterization: if $S$ is not zero forcing, does some field and nonzero directed weighting admit a nonzero harmonic function vanishing on $S$? This would identify zero forcing exactly with universal weighted harmonic uniqueness.

\section{Conclusion}
The unique-uncolored-neighbor condition has two roles. Combinatorially it makes the next color deterministic; algebraically it reduces a neighbor equation to one nonzero coefficient times one unknown. It follows that forcing is strictly growing and irreversible, triangle and diamond rules provide composable local certificates, and every zero forcing set is a uniqueness set for every nondegenerate weighted neighbor-sum system over every field. Degree-three domination, cubic parity, and triangle-unit parity provide complementary global constraints for future structural and extremal analysis.

\end{document}

